"use strict";(self.webpackChunkdatabricks_monolith=self.webpackChunkdatabricks_monolith||[]).push([[22029],{322029:e=>{e.exports='# Published Data Sources (`.tds` / `.tdsx`)\n\nAbout 22% of customer workbooks reference a **published data source** (Tableau Server / Online artifact) via a `sqlproxy` placeholder. The `.twb` itself does not carry the real connection, table paths, Custom SQL, or calculated fields — those live in a companion `.tds` / `.tdsx` file. Without resolving the TDS, the metric view\'s `source:` ends up as a `sqlproxy` placeholder and any Custom SQL is missing.\n\nUse this skill when migrating a workbook that contains a `sqlproxy` datasource.\n\n## Detection\n\nThe extractor surfaces published-DS references at two layers — use the cheaper one first.\n\n**Workbook-level (cheap signal).** Read `features.datasource_features`:\n\n- `has_published_datasource_refs: true` — the workbook references at least one published data source.\n- `published_datasources_count: N` — how many distinct ones (also reflected in `features.datasource_features.connection_types.published_datasource`).\n\nIf both report zero, no TDS file is needed for this workbook.\n\n**Per-datasource (which DSs need a TDS).** Walk `datasources[]` and flag any entry where **either** of these holds (both are independently sufficient):\n\n- some `connections[].connection_type` equals `sqlproxy` — the canonical signal driven by the workbook XML `connection class="sqlproxy"` attribute, or\n- `name` starts with `sqlproxy.` (e.g. `sqlproxy.0abc12d34ef`) — the legacy / aliased form.\n\nThe two usually appear together, but trust either alone. As a confirming side-effect, every value in such a DS\'s `column_table_map` will be the literal string `"sqlproxy"` and `connections[].properties.dbname` will carry the published-DS id used as the linking key (see "Linking" below) — these are useful for diagnostics but not the primary detection signal.\n\nIf the workbook has any published-DS reference and **no** corresponding published-DS file is in the session (the agent state\'s `filenames` list will surface them once parsed), stop and ask the user for the UC Volume path(s) before proceeding. Accept paths mid-session — the workbook does not need to be re-parsed.\n\nA workbook may reference multiple published data sources; ask for one UC Volume path per reference.\n\n## Reading the parsed TDS\n\n`parseDashboard` writes two files per published data source to the session:\n\n- `<basename>.tds.json` — structured metadata (read via `queryMetadata`).\n- `<basename>.tds` — raw inner `.tds` XML (XPath/regex fallback for anything not surfaced in the JSON).\n\n### Array entities\n\n| Entity              | Contents                                                                              |\n| ------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `columns`           | Remote column names, parent table names, ODBC + Tableau types (also see `relations`). |\n| `relations`         | Physical tables (`type: "table"`) or Custom SQL bodies (`type: "text"`).              |\n| `calculated_fields` | Tableau-formula calculated fields that live on the published DS, not in the workbook. |\n| `object_graph`      | Logical layer table positions (`object_id`, `caption`, `table_path`).                 |\n| `warnings`          | Parser-emitted warnings about malformed or unexpected TDS structure.                  |\n\n### Root-level fields\n\n| Entity                | Contents                                                                                      |\n| --------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `repository_location` | Object with `id` (the linking key, see below), `site`, `revision`, `path`.                    |\n| `connection`          | Object with `connection_class` (source dialect), `catalog`, `schema_name`, `server`, …        |\n| `connection_class`    | Convenience string of the inner connection class (`redshift`, `snowflake`, `sqlserver`, …).   |\n| `custom_sql`          | Top-level Custom SQL body when present; otherwise `null`. Also surfaces in `relations[]`.     |\n| `column_table_map`    | `{ remote_column_name: parent_table_name }` for resolving which table each column belongs to. |\n| `has_extract`         | `true` when the TDS is backed by a Hyper extract.                                             |\n| `source_file`         | The original `.tds`/`.tdsx` filename.                                                         |\n\n### Schemas\n\n#### `columns[]`\n\nA column exposed by the published data source.\n\n- `remote_name` — column name in the source DB (e.g. `sale_month`).\n- `parent_name` — relation the column belongs to (e.g. `dim_product`, or `Custom SQL Query` for text relations). Brackets stripped.\n- `local_name` — Tableau-internal reference (`[sale_month]`); may differ from `remote_name`.\n- `remote_type` — ODBC type code (integer).\n- `local_type` — Tableau type — `integer` | `string` | `real` | `date` | `datetime` | `boolean`.\n\n#### `relations[]`\n\nA physical table or Custom SQL body.\n\n- `name` — relation name (e.g. `sales_fact`, or `Custom SQL Query` for `text` relations).\n- `table` — 3-part bracketed path (`[main].[tds_test].[sales_fact]`) for `type: "table"`; the literal `(Custom SQL)` for `type: "text"`.\n- `type` — `table` | `text`.\n- `connection_ref` — named-connection reference when the TDS declares multiple connections; otherwise `null`.\n\n#### `calculated_fields[]`\n\nA calculated field that lives on the published DS (not in the workbook).\n\n- `name` — internal Tableau identifier (`[Calculation_XXX]`).\n- `caption` — display label (e.g. `Revenue`).\n- `formula` — Tableau formula text.\n- `datatype` — Tableau datatype string.\n- `role` — `dimension` | `measure` | `null` (absent attribute preserved as `null`).\n\n#### `object_graph[]`\n\nAn entry in the logical layer\'s object graph.\n\n- `object_id` — internal object id.\n- `caption` — display name.\n- `table_path` — logical path to the underlying relation(s).\n\n#### `repository_location` (root)\n\n- `id` — the linking key against the workbook\'s sqlproxy DS (see "Linking" below).\n- `path` — server path (e.g. `/t/site/datasources`).\n- `site` — Tableau Server / Online site name.\n- `revision` — datasource revision number.\n\n#### `connection` (root)\n\n- `connection_class` — source dialect (`databricks`, `redshift`, `snowflake`, `sqlserver`, …).\n- `catalog` — UC catalog / DB name.\n- `schema_name` — schema name.\n- `server` — connection server URL.\n- `warehouse_path` — Databricks SQL warehouse path; non-null only for Databricks / Spark dialects.\n\n## Linking a TDS to a workbook datasource\n\nThe TDS\'s `repository_location.id` matches the workbook\'s sqlproxy datasource by `connection.properties.dbname` (or, on some older TDS files, `connections[0].properties.dbname`).\n\nWhen you have multiple `.tds.json` files in the session, iterate over each, read its `repository_location.id`, and match it against each workbook sqlproxy DS\'s `dbname`. If two TDS files claim the same `repository_location.id` but differ in `site` or `revision`, **ask the user which one to use** rather than guessing.\n\n## Mixed federated + sqlproxy workbooks\n\nMigrate each workbook datasource in isolation. Only apply TDS data to datasources whose `name` starts with `sqlproxy.` — federated DS (any other name) already have real connection info in the TWB and must be migrated from the workbook metadata alone.\n\n## Custom SQL passthrough\n\nWhen a TDS has `relations[]` with `type == "text"` and a non-empty `custom_sql`, the migrated metric view\'s `source:` must be that Custom SQL body, **dialect-converted to Databricks SQL**. Use `connection.connection_class` as the dialect hint:\n\n- `redshift`: `::DATE` → `CAST(... AS DATE)`; `nvl` → `COALESCE`; `getdate()` → `current_timestamp()`; `DATEADD(\'day\', n, col)` → `col + INTERVAL n DAY`.\n- `snowflake`: `IFF` → `IF`; `TRY_TO_DATE` → `TRY_CAST(... AS DATE)`; `:: TYPE` casts → `CAST(... AS TYPE)`.\n- `sqlserver`: `ISNULL` → `COALESCE`; `GETDATE()` → `current_timestamp()`; `TOP n` → `LIMIT n` (and remove from inner subqueries that don\'t support it).\n- `databricks`: pass through unchanged.\n- Anything else: surface the dialect to the user and ask whether to skip or to attempt a best-effort translation.\n\n**Do not summarise, reformat, or rewrite the SQL beyond dialect-specific syntax.** Preserve column lists, aliases, joins, and filter predicates verbatim.\n\n## Real table paths\n\nWhen a TDS has `relations[]` with `type == "table"`, use `relations[].table` (3-part `[catalog].[schema].[table]`) as the metric view `source:`. Strip the brackets and join with dots — e.g. `[main].[analytics].[orders]` → `main.analytics.orders`.\n\nUse `column_table_map` to resolve which fields belong to which table when the workbook references multiple TDS tables.\n\n## Calculated field merge\n\nIf the TDS has `calculated_fields[]` whose `name` is referenced by a worksheet but not present in the workbook\'s datasource `columns`, treat them as additional fields when generating metric view measures/dimensions. Translate their `formula` per `calc_to_sql.md` and apply the same skip-criteria decision tree from `SKILL.md`.\n\n## Skip cases\n\nA TDS is **unusable for migration** when any of the following holds. Skip the entire workbook (or the affected datasource) and record the reason in the metric view\'s top-level `comment:` field:\n\n- `connection.connection_class` is `excel`, `textscan` (CSV), `json`, or `xml` — file-based, no SQL target.\n- `connection` is `null` and only `has_extract: true` is set — extract-only Hyper file, no live connection.\n- `relations[]` is empty after parsing — no usable tables or SQL.\n\n## Dialect tagging\n\nAlways include in the metric view `comment:` field which source connection class was used, so future maintainers know a dialect translation happened:\n\n```yaml\ncomment: |\n  Sourced from published data source (original connection_class: redshift).\n  Skipped calculations:\n  - ...\n```\n'}}]);
